Subject: register VM: fuse a multiply into the add or subtract that consumes it

What this does:
  A multiply whose product is one of the operands of the immediately following
  add or subtract is lowered to a single instruction that performs both: it
  forms the product, stores it to the multiply's destination, and writes the
  sum or difference to the add's destination. The fused instruction still
  writes the product, so anything that reads it afterwards sees exactly what it
  saw before; what is saved is one dispatch and the second instruction's operand
  decode. No single-use condition is needed for that reason.

  Both operand positions are covered, and they are four distinct opcodes rather
  than one with a swapped operand list: `c + p` is not `p + c` when a string is
  involved, because concatenation order is observable, and `c - p` is not
  `p - c` at all. The two orders are ROP_mul_add/ROP_mul_sub (`p` on the left)
  and ROP_add_mul/ROP_sub_mul (`p` on the right).

  The add must read its other operand after the product is stored, because that
  operand may be the multiply's own destination; the float path therefore forms
  the product, stores it, and only then reads the third operand. The float case
  multiplies and adds in one expression and never round-trips through a JSValue;
  the integer case chains regvm_num_fast; anything else -- a string, an object, a
  mixed pair -- continues in a slow handler that runs the two operations in
  order through js_regvm_binary.

  The fusion pass runs beside the chained-read fusion, after register allocation
  and the ownership analysis and after dead-store elision. It matches the
  unlowered ROP_binary form, because the rewrite from ROP_binary to the specific
  arithmetic opcode happens later, in the encoder. It fires only on IR-adjacent
  pairs, refuses a branch, catch or finally target and a block leader at the
  second instruction -- dropping it would otherwise move a target past the fused
  pair -- and can be disabled with REGVM_NOARITH2.

Why we need it:
  On box2d the arithmetic pairs are the largest dynamic adjacency that fusion
  can address: mul->add is 3.97 M and mul->sub 2.37 M of the 64.76 M adjacent
  dispatch transitions, and the product is usually the right operand of the add
  (the common source shape is `acc + a*b`). Fusion is the remaining
  non-structural lever, and the multiply feeding the very next operation is its
  densest target.

Adds:
  Four register-VM opcodes (ROP_mul_add, ROP_mul_sub, ROP_add_mul, ROP_sub_mul)
  with format "rrrrr" -- destination-of-the-multiply, two multiply operands, the
  add's destination, the add's other operand -- their threaded handlers and slow
  continuations, their monolith labels, and the fusion pass that produces them
  from an adjacent ROP_binary multiply and add/subtract.

Evidence:
  Box2D 32-iteration `fixed.js`: 29.055 G to 28.746 G instructions
  (**-1.06 %**, -0.309 G), cycles 13.103 G to 12.766 G (**-2.57 %**), wall
  -2.4 %. On `check.js` the pass fuses 6,292,072 dispatches (ROP_mul_add
  17,708, ROP_add_mul 3,948,024, ROP_sub_mul 2,326,340), matching the predicted
  6.34 M mul->add/sub pairs, which is about 7 instructions saved per pair.
  Checksum `546.1008391043414` exact; `QJS_REGVM_VERIFY=1` build clean; the
  differential suite passes 398/0/0 in both mono and threaded modes; hand
  kernels cover both operand positions, both operators, integer and float, and
  the order-sensitive string-concatenation slow path.

Bytecode bump:
  No. The four opcodes are appended at the end of the enum so every existing
  opcode keeps its value, and they are never emitted by the encoder -- they
  exist only between the fusion pass and the encoder within a single compile --
  so no blob contains them.

